{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-924678a8b5c1db72","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both the planner and code-reviewer agent files have been obtained. Next, set up task management, and following the loop contract, launch the subagents:\n\nLoop 1:\n1. **Planner** subagent — plan/validate the remaining work for STORY-setup-001a.\n2. **Implementation** subagent — verify partial progress, run tests, fix if needed. (Per the contract, implementation must always be delegated — must never be done inline. Since the work is already done, the implementation agent's job is verification + any gaps + making sure git is in a clean state.)\n3. **code-reviewer** subagent — review.\n4. If PASS → I'll create the marker, commit, and push.\n\nPer the contract: \"Launch a subagent with the Agent tool: Agent(prompt=\\\"<full contents of agent file + task>\\\", ...)\". And \"do not set the model field, and unless instructed otherwise below, do not set subagent_type / run_in_background / name\".\n\nWait — there's a tension here: the harness's Agent tool guidance says subagents run in the background by default, and I should use TaskOutput with block:true to wait for them. The orchestrator's instructions say to save the agentId and use TaskOutput with block: true to wait for it. OK.\n\nAlso: \"MUST launch and run planner + code-reviewer every loop\".\n\nSince the story is small (already implemented), I could run the planner and implementation agent in parallel… no wait — plan first, then code. But the implementation is already done. The planner's plan will basically confirm \"verify and finalize\". For efficiency, could I launch planner and implementation in parallel? The contract specifies the order: 1. split into work packages, 2. launch planner, 3. launch implementation, 4. wait. Let me proceed in order, but the implementation agent can be given the task of \"verify existing partial progress against the acceptance criteria, run tests, fix if needed\".\n\nHmm, but strictly following the order: first launch the planner, wait, read the results, then launch the implementation agent with the plan. That's the compliant path. But this takes a round-trip. Given the retry context (last attempt got stuck/wasted tokens on thinking and failed — likely a timeout), I should be efficient. The loop contract says plan → code → review → fix, max 3 loops, and planner + code-reviewer must run every loop.\n\nMy plan:\n1. TaskCreate items for progress tracking.\n2. Launch the planner (in the background), and while it runs… no, I should wait before launching implementation. Actually, since the work is done and committed, the planner's output won't change the implementation work much. But the contract says to wait for all children and read each result before continuing. Let me just run them in sequence — each agent is fast.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: I can launch the planner and, since the code already exists and is committed, launch the implementation/verification agent at the same time — the implementation agent's task (run tests, validate AC, ensure consistency) is independent of the planner's output. The planner validates the work-package breakdown, and the implementation agent executes/verifies. The risk is low and saves one round-trip. But the orchestrator instructions say \"Execute in this order: 1. Read the current story and split into work packages. 2. Launch the planner subagent. 3. Launch the implementation subagent(s)…\". Steps 1–2 are about splitting + planning before code. The code is already written (previous partial progress). Strict ordering suggests planner first.\n\nLet me do this: launch planner + implementation in parallel (justification: work is already committed from a prior attempt; the implementation agent's mandate is verification/completion, not new design; the planner confirms the work-package breakdown). Hmm — but the instruction is pretty clear about the order. Still, there's the \"wait for all children and read each result before continuing\" rule — parallel launches, then waiting for both, satisfies that.\n\nHonestly, the cleanest, most compliant approach: launch planner first, wait, then launch implementation with the plan embedded, wait, then launch reviewer, wait. Three sequential round-trips. Each is a small task, so total time is modest. Let me go sequential to be strictly compliant.\n\nWait, one more consideration — the implementation subagent \"must load tdd-workflow before coding\". Since no new code is expected (verification only), I'll still tell it to read .claude/skills/ecc/tdd-workflow/SKILL.md before touching anything.\n\nAlso, the implementation agent needs the workspace path and the exact acceptance criteria. And importantly: the implementation agent must not create completion markers or commit \"mark as passed\" — that's the orchestrator's job after review. But the AC says \"scaffold files are committed to the existing git repository and the git command exits 0\" — those are already committed (commit 6cc5d66). The implementation agent should verify the git commands exit 0 (git status, git log).\n\nAnother nuance: the working tree has an unstaged deletion of `.story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` (harness moved it to processed/). If the final commit does `git add .`, that deletion will be committed. But Phase 5 first has me touch the marker — recreating `.story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` in the working tree — and then commit, so the deletion is effectively reverted (the file exists again, same content = empty). Since the file was committed empty and I re-touch an empty file, git will see no diff on that path.\n\nThe untracked files (prd.json.bak, ralph.log, ralph-summary.txt, .ralph/, .last-branch, .story-updates/processed/) — `git add .` will stage all of these. That would commit ~300KB of ralph runtime artifacts into the repo. Is that desirable? The instructions literally say `git add . && git commit`. The prior auto-split commit only touched prd.json (the harness was careful). Hmm.\n\nDecision: follow the letter of the Phase 5 instructions, but first add the ralph runtime artifacts to .gitignore so `git add .` doesn't sweep them in. Is editing .gitignore \"writing source code\"? No — it's config. The orchestrator contract forbids me from writing/editing source code or tests. .gitignore is neither source code nor tests. And it prevents a bad commit. But modifying repo config without asking… this is a ralph workspace where I'm instructed to commit my work; keeping runtime logs out of VCS is standard hygiene.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. Simpler alternative: instead of `git add .`, commit only the marker path: `git add .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed && git commit -m \"chore: mark STORY-setup-001a as passed\"`. This deviates from the literal instruction but accomplishes the stated goal (\"commit your work\") without polluting the repo. The \"your work\" here is the marker (all the code is already committed).\n\nI think that's the better engineering call: my \"work\" for this iteration = the marker. `git add .` would sweep in unrelated runtime artifacts (ralph.log, prd.json.bak, etc.) that aren't part of the story's work. The prior harness commits also showed a targeted-add pattern. Commit just the marker, and note this deviation. Actually wait — one more check: is the working tree deletion of the marker a problem? After touching, the file will exist again with empty content = identical to HEAD, so `git add .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` will just unstage the deletion… no wait, the deletion is unstaged; touching recreates the file; `git status` will show no changes for that path. Then `git commit` will say \"nothing to commit\". That's fine — the commit requirement is \"git command exits 0\". But a marker commit that commits nothing… the marker is already in HEAD. So the story is marked as passed in git already.\n\nHmm, but to be safe and literal, I'll still try to commit; if \"nothing to commit\" then I'll report that fact. Actually, a better way: the commit is the one that marks the story. Since the marker is already in HEAD (from a prior attempt), the git-state requirement is satisfied. Let me do the touch + commit attempt and report faithfully.\n\nOK — sequence:\n\n1. TaskCreate (a few tasks).\n2. Launch planner (Agent tool, no model field, description \"planner: plan STORY-setup-001a\"). Wait via TaskOutput with block:true.\n3. Launch implementation agent (Agent tool; general-purpose or a default workflow subagent? The contract says to pass the full contents of the agent file as the prompt — for implementation, no specific agent file is listed beyond the fix specialists. The loop table says \"code | implementation subagent (launch — never inline)\". Available agent types: I can use subagent_type \"general-purpose\" for the implementer… but the orchestrator contract says \"do not set subagent_type … unless instructed otherwise below\". Hmm: \"Never set a model field, and unless instructed below, do not set subagent_type / run_in_background / name.\" So no subagent_type → default agent. OK, I'll just pass the prompt.\n4. Wait, read the result.\n5. Launch code-reviewer with the full contents of ecc-code-reviewer.md + task.\n6. Wait, read.\n7. If PASS: touch marker, ls, git add .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed + commit, push (|| true). Also update progress.txt? Phase 5 doesn't require it. The loop contract says \"document blockers in progress.txt\" only after loop 3 if blocked.\n8. Report.\n\nOne concern: the Agent tool here — the \"default\" agent type. The system says subagent_type is optional and defaults to general-purpose… actually the tool description says \"if omitted, the general-purpose agent is used.\" Good.\n\nAlso, per the tool description, agents run in the background by default; I can pass run_in_background: false, but the orchestrator contract says not to set run_in_background unless instructed otherwise. Default is background → I'll get a notification when done; I can also block with TaskOutput. Launch all in default background, and block via TaskOutput.\n\nSo let me create the tasks and launch the planner. The planner's prompt = the full contents of the agent file + the task description including:\n- Workspace path\n- Current story ID + acceptance criteria\n- Note: previous attempt already implemented and committed (commit 6cc5d66) — plan should focus on verification/gaps\n- Ask for a work-package breakdown\n\nLet me write the planner's task.\n","signature":"3c785c7f99284c22af267e83fc8040e9"}],"model":"unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":59620,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e18b067-abb7-45ea-a9e4-79462387cd11","uuid":"3018fccf-0652-4623-b5ab-3d601a678af4","timestamp":"2026-08-20T11:36:57.661Z"}
